  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\n**** Scattered Storms This Afternoon ****\n\nThe severe thunderstorm complex that rolled across northern\nIllinois this morning has pushed an outflow boundary southward\nto the I-74 corridor this afternoon. As expected, scattered\nthunderstorms are developing along this boundary. The atmosphere\nacross central Illinois is presently moderately unstable\n(MLCAPEs 2000-3000J/kg), but weakly capped thanks to very warm\nair aloft. The RAP shows the cap gradually weakening as the\nafternoon progresses and MLCAPEs increasing to around 4000J/kg\nalong/east of the I-57 corridor after 21z/4pm. As a result,\nthink the narrow line of storms currently ongoing along a\nHoopeston to Bellflower line will intensify as it drops\nsoutheastward...posing a risk of marginally severe wind and\nhail. Will continue to monitor cells between Peoria and\nBloomington as well, but think the main location for a few\nwind/hail reports through 6pm will be along/northeast of a\nLincoln to Paris line. \n\n\n**** Storm Complex on Wednesday ****\n\nAfter a mostly quiet night, a short-wave trough ejecting out of\nthe Rockies will trigger another cluster of thunderstorms\nacross Iowa late tonight into Wednesday morning. With the\neffective boundary pushed a bit further south than it has been\nover the past few days, it appears these storms will take a more\nsoutherly route E/SE across the heart of central Illinois. CAMs\nexhibit a high degree of variability, so confidence in the\nexact location/timing of the storms remains low. Given the\nprevailing synoptic pattern and a persistence forecast, think\nthe 12z RRFS offers the most likely scenario...taking storms\ninto the Illinois River Valley by late morning, then further\neast across the rest of the area during the afternoon.\n\nThe environment feeding into the storm complex will not be as\nunstable or sheared as today's scenario across northern\nIllinois. The 12z HREF suggests ensemble mean SBCAPEs of\n2000-2500J/kg (rather than the 3000-4000J/kg from today), while\nthe GFS/NAM generally show 0-6km bulk shear of 25-35kt. These\nparameters are certainly supportive of strong to severe\nconvection: however, am not anticipating the same magnitude of \ntoday's storms to the north. Instead think damaging wind gusts \nof 60-70mph, hail larger than quarters, and isolated tornadoes \nwill be the main hazards.\n\n\n**** Additional Storm Chances Through Friday ****\n\nThe latest SPC Day 3 Convective Outlook highlights central\nIllinois with a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) on Thursday. With\nthe boundary still wavering in the immediate vicinity,\nadditional storm clusters will form and track across the region\nWednesday night through Thursday night. Given an overall\ndecrease in deep-layer shear, the severe risk will gradually\nwane as well. Gusty winds, hail, and locally heavy downpours\nwill be likely on Thursday. After that, the prevailing upper\nridge currently centered over the Desert Southwest will shift\ninto the lower Mississippi River Valley by the end of the week.\nWith rising upper heights, the surface frontal boundary will get\nshunted back northward and storm chances will decrease by Friday\nand Saturday.",
